1. You and a remote teammate have unfortunately edited the same line of code in main.py at the same time. The version control system is now confused about which version to keep.
Mission: Simulating this crisis locally, you must force Git into a "Merge Conflict" state to understand how the system pauses deployment.
Setup: Create a new branch named 'simulation' specifically for this exercise.
Trigger: Attempt to merge simulation into main.

Assessment Data:

1. Execution Log: List the sequence of commands you executed to create this crisis from scratch:

•git status
•git switch -c simulation
•git add main.py
•git commit -m "Change main.py on simulation"
•git switch main
•git add main.py
•git commit -m "Change main.py on main"
•git merge simulation


2. System Artifact: Open the conflicted file. Transcribe the exact string Git generated between the HEAD marker and the separator.

Conflict Text: print("Login Feature Enabled - MAIN") 

2. A critical vulnerability has been discovered in the legacy code. Management requires a report detailing exactly who wrote the very first line of the application and when it was committed, to establish a chain of custody.
Mission: Use the repository's forensic tools to inspect the line-by-line history of main.py without opening the text editor.

Assessment Data:

1. Which specific command provides a line-by-line historical annotation?

Command: git blame main.py

